## 1. The idea in one paragraph

An individual learning agent can pass every stability test we know how to run
and still help destabilize the market it operates in. When adaptive agents
share an environment that reacts to their decisions - dealers whose quotes
reshape a common pool of informed flow, recommenders competing for one
attention pool, pricers facing one demand curve - each agent's retraining
changes the data every *other* agent will learn from next. That coupling is a
**learning externality**: no agent internalizes the effect of its own
adaptation on the environment its competitors face, and feedback that is
harmless in one agent compounds across many. Two questions follow, and the
paper answers both. *What sets the strength of the externality?* Not the
number of firms but the **effective number of independent learners**, a
spectral measure of how aligned the firms' feedback directions are: fifty
dealers fine-tuning one vendor's foundation model are dynamically close to
one very large learner, so foundation-model concentration enters the
stability condition as a term rather than as a talking point. *What can be
done about it?* Three closed-form levers, all expressed in that one quantity:
a **crowding-cadence frontier** saying how much slower everyone must retrain
as the market's effective crowding rises, and the crowding level past which
no cadence helps; a **herd-immunity threshold** saying the market is stable
iff the fraction of agents running feedback-aware corrected updates exceeds
`1 - 1/m_N`, exactly the epidemiological `1 - 1/R_0` law with the systemic
modulus as the reproduction number; and a **Pigouvian wedge** pricing the
externality. The synthesis result is that the last two trade off against each
other: **model diversity and corrected learning are substitutes**, and the
paper gives the frontier along which a market can buy stability with either.

